 ### [Coarse-to-fine multi-resolution densification](15569-coarse-to-fine-multi-resolution-densification.md)
-Allocates iterations based on image frequency energy to decouple global coverage from local refinement.
+Allocates iterations across resolutions based on frequency energy to decouple global spread from local refinement.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Standard 3DGS optimization complexity, plus overhead for mesh proximity queries and normal estimation for $G^t$ [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for mesh geometry and separate tracking for $G^t$ and $G^l$ [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Evaluated on Mip-NeRF360 and Deep Blending datasets [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Aligns Gaussians with explicit mesh geometry, preventing expansion into free space [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the input mesh; tightly-bound Gaussians are constrained by mesh topology [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When an explicit mesh is available and high-fidelity alignment with surface geometry is required [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When no mesh is available or when the scene contains significant non-rigid deformation not captured by the mesh [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Similar to 3DGS, dominated by rendering and gradient computation. The additional mesh queries (closest face, point sampling) add overhead proportional to the number of tightly-bound splats [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the mesh geometry alongside the Gaussian attributes. The mesh size depends on the scene complexity [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Evaluated on Mip-NeRF360 and Deep Blending datasets. Trained for 30k iterations [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Aligns Gaussians with explicit geometry, reducing artifacts and improving reconstruction quality on surfaces [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Dependent on the quality of the input mesh. The projection loss formula ambiguity may lead to suboptimal convergence if not corrected [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in **MeshGS: Adaptive Mesh-Aligned Gaussian Splatting for High-Quality Rendering** [S1]. It builds on the original 3DGS densification strategy [S1] by introducing mesh-aware constraints. The method distinguishes between tightly-bound and loosely-bound splats to balance mesh alignment and general rendering flexibility [S1].
+MeshGS (Adaptive Mesh-Aligned Gaussian Splatting) was introduced to address the limitation of 3DGS in ignoring explicit 3D geometry [S1]. It builds on the original 3DGS densification strategy but adapts it for tightly-bound splats by using mesh-based sampling for cloning and splitting [S1]. The method was evaluated against LTM and SuGaR, demonstrating improved rendering quality [S1].
 
